# OPTIONAL for local development.
#
# scripts/fetch-content.mjs reads `supabase status` by itself, so with the
# local stack running there is nothing to put here and no key to copy:
#
#     supabase start
#     npm run check
#
# This file is for the cases where there is no CLI to ask: a hosted project,
# or the Cloudflare build (where these become build-environment variables).
#
#     SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
#     SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...
#
# Build time only. Nothing here reaches the browser -- `npm run verify:build`
# fails if a JWT-shaped string appears anywhere in dist/. The anon key is
# public by design; RLS is what protects the data, which is exactly why the
# build uses it: a broken policy fails the build instead of leaking.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database connection strings, for `npm run check:db`.
#
# These are NOT like the publishable key above. A publishable key is public by
# design and RLS protects the data behind it; these carry the database password
# and grant full access. .env is gitignored -- keep it that way, keep it mode
# 600, and never paste one of these into a chat, an issue or a commit.
#
# Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string -> URI. Use the SESSION
# POOLER form: the direct `db.<ref>.supabase.co` host is IPv6-only on some
# networks and will simply hang.
#
#     DEV_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres.hjsekipqryfuwdkhxuks:PASSWORD@aws-1-eu-west-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#     PROD_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres.eqcgeqzzuzcwrflwasjo:PASSWORD@aws-1-eu-west-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# scripts/check-db.sh PARSES this file rather than sourcing it -- a password is
# exactly the kind of string that contains $ and backticks -- and reads only
# these two keys. An exported environment variable wins over the file.
#
# With them set, `npm run check` also checks the database. Without them it
# prints a nine-line SKIPPED banner, which is the only thing standing between
# this and a check nobody runs.
